    "label": "E",
    "text": "Finally, there is the question of replacements. Few new antibiotic classes have reached the market in recent decades, because the economics are unattractive: a drug taken for a week earns far less than one taken for life, and stewardship rules deliberately limit its use. Proposals include subscription payments, in which governments pay companies for access rather than volume, and renewed support for research into alternatives such as bacteriophages."
